#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179459 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5b5c517-8e13-5b70-bd1f-15ae495f0632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/id #uuid "1cadcaa9-876e-56cb-b8b3-17695827c4f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-7035" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C \\dfrac{y\\,\\mathrm{d}x - x\\,\\mathrm{d}y}{x^2+y^2}$, where $C$ is a part of the circle $x^2+y^2=4$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $\\left(0,2\\right)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3703/Source/Images/3b681a72da2d77baebf38cb87698a572.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that a parametrization for the given path is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= x(t)i + y(t)j = 2\\cos ti + 2\\sin tj, t ∈ [0, \\frac{π}{2}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= x^{′}(t)i + y^{′}(t)j =-2\\sin ti + 2\\cos tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)\\frac{ydx - xdy}{x^{2} + y^{2}} &= ∫_{0}^{π/2}\\frac{y \\cdot x^{′} - x \\cdot y^{′}}{x^{2} + y^{2}}dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π/2}((2\\sin t)(-2\\sin t) - (2\\cos t)(2\\cos t))/((2\\cos t)^{2} + (2\\sin t)^{2}), dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π/2}(-4\\sin^{2} t - 4\\cos^{2} t)/(4(\\cos^{2} t + \\sin^{2} t))dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π/2}(-4(\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t))/(4(\\cos^{2} t + \\sin^{2} t))dt \\\\\n&= - ∫_{0}^{π/2}dt \\\\\n&=-\\frac{π}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179460 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f04a969-e7e0-592f-b5cd-e92fedb217a8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Cartesian Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "5a63ca22-0b1a-5584-8b43-c80457016cd3" :question/math-academy-id "e-7038" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y=x^2$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to the point $B(1,1)$, evaluate the line integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C (x+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x + (x-y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y = x^2$. As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t) = x(t)i + y(t)j = ti + t^{2}j, t ∈ [0, 1]\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j = i + 2tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)(x + y)dx + (x - y)dy &= ∫_{0}^{1}(x + y)\\frac{dx}{dt}dt + (x - y)\\frac{dy}{dt}dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}((x + y)\\frac{dx}{dt} + (x - y)\\frac{dy}{dt})dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(t + t^{2}) \\cdot 1 + (t - t^{2}) \\cdot 2tdt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}t + 3t^{2} - 2t^{3}dt \\\\\n&= [\\frac{t^{2}}{2} + t^{3} - \\frac{t^{4}}{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= [\\frac{1}{2} + 1 - \\frac{1}{2}] - [0] \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179520 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "223ec615-045d-543e-96f4-fec8882bec9e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Over Segments"}] :question/id #uuid "9d13f401-5e07-5b07-8916-df2b0b6b8f74" :question/math-academy-id "e-9515" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overline{AB}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to the point $B(1, 1)$, calculate the line integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C x^2y\\,\\mathrm{d}x + xy\\,\\mathrm{d}y$." :question/worked-solution "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = 0\\,\\mathbf{i} + 0\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = 1\\,\\mathbf{i} + 1\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= a + t(b - a) \\\\\n&= ti + tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n0 \\le t \\le 1\n$$\n\nSo, we have that $x(t) = t$ and $y(t) = t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j = i + j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)x^{2}ydx + xydy &= ∫_{0}^{1}x^{2}y\\frac{dx}{dt}dt + xy\\frac{dy}{dt}dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(x^{2}y\\frac{dx}{dt} + xy\\frac{dy}{dt})dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}t^{2} \\cdot t \\cdot 1 + t \\cdot t \\cdot 1dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}t^{3} + t^{2}dt \\\\\n&= [\\frac{t^{4}}{4} + \\frac{t^{3}}{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179939 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5b5c517-8e13-5b70-bd1f-15ae495f0632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f634e5b-311d-5dbb-bc6b-6479c6ebd90b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- ∫_{0}^{π/2}\\sin^{3} t + \\cos^{3} tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177635} {:answer/id #uuid "a5b1234d-e54e-5884-aa75-37a3b8f0a962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{π/2}\\sin^{2} t\\cos t + \\cos^{2} t\\sin tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177636} {:answer/id #uuid "a7de1e1a-6f20-501b-b12e-ba07d971dae7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{π/2}\\sin^{2} t\\cos tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177637} {:answer/id #uuid "b693492f-4da3-5a57-b80a-880971bee015" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{π/2}\\frac{1}{2}\\sin 2tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177638} {:answer/id #uuid "4dbcc0a9-71b5-5769-91f7-a99509737a54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- ∫_{0}^{π/2}\\sin^{3} tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f634e5b-311d-5dbb-bc6b-6479c6ebd90b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- ∫_{0}^{π/2}\\sin^{3} t + \\cos^{3} tdt" :db/id #edb/